# id  tag  kind  t_s  alt_km  L_si_W  flag  note
1 - first_light 0.0030 100.000 5.3791e+03 info  L_si first exceeds 1e-6 of its peak. This is just a visibility threshold; the real value depends on your sensor floor and on where the simulation starts (zstart).
2 A ablation_onset 1.3050 80.825 1.5591e+05 stylized  Net heating flux first exceeds radiative loss at T_ablate = 2300 K, so ablation switches on (a kink in the slope). Real onset is gradual over a few km. BELOW this point the model's light is drag work only (no ablated material): treat light above ~81 km as low-confidence. Loosely like the end of Phase 1 / start of Phase 2 in [R22], but the model has no spin, volatile (Na/K) emission or melt physics.
3 Y first_yield 3.0060 55.771 1.0486e+07 physical  Loading rho v^2 reaches S0 = 0.12 MPa. The body starts to flatten (pancake): area, drag and heating all grow, so the light rises faster. Analogue of the onset of fragmentation (Phase 5 / H_f in [R22]).
4 F flare 3.0466 55.175 2.7926e+07 stylized  Light rises x6.9 in 0.15 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: yield gen 0 at 55.7 km (100% of m0); split gen 0 at 54.7 km (99% of m0). Dust groups supply 49% of the rise and 42% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
5 B first_breakup 3.0750 54.757 4.9091e+07 stylized  Spread factor reached f_split = 2: the body breaks into 4 children plus 20 % of its mass as dust. The dust has a large area-to-mass ratio, decelerates and ablates within a fraction of a second, which makes a sharp flare. The instantaneous release is a model simplification. Light grew x4.7 between first yield and this breakup (pancake spreading).
6 - drag_overtakes_ablation 4.0110 41.332 2.1418e+08 stylized  Drag work now exceeds the kinetic energy carried off by ablated mass. The ratio ablation/drag is about Lambda v^2/(2 Q Cd), so this happens when the speed falls below ~14 km/s (from the model's heating law).
7 F flare 4.1593 39.220 4.3128e+08 stylized  Light rises x2.8 in 0.10 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: yield gen 1 at 39.3 km (33% of m0); yield gen 1 at 39.3 km (17% of m0); yield gen 1 at 39.3 km (11% of m0) +5 more. Dust groups supply 19% of the rise and 21% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
8 P peak 4.1793 38.936 7.3298e+08 physical  Maximum light. It sits ~+0.7 km from the peak of energy deposition (38.2 km) because the luminous efficiency tau(v) falls as the fragments slow (mass-weighted speed here 13.9 km/s).
9 D rolloff 4.5033 35.030 2.6801e+08 physical  Light has fallen to 1/e of the peak 0.32 s after it. Cause: the kinetic energy is being used up (speed 73% of v0, 58% of the mass still in flight) and tau(v) drops with speed. Analogue of Phase 7 (decline until dark flight) in [R22].
10 R retirement_step 5.6223 27.472 1.0078e+06 artifact  A group is removed because it slowed below v_dark = 3.0 km/s; the light drops 16% within 0.03 s. A bookkeeping step, not a physical dimming.
11 R retirement_step 5.6493 27.310 8.6247e+05 artifact  A group is removed because it slowed below v_dark = 3.0 km/s; the light drops 17% within 0.03 s. A bookkeeping step, not a physical dimming.
12 T tail 5.6733 26.593 7.2362e+05 low-confidence  Below 1e-3 of the peak the light is controlled by groups being retired when they slow below v_dark = 3.0 km/s and by the tau taper (2-4 km/s). Do not interpret structure below this level.
